WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.500
Hello, everyone! Welcome to the Langfocus channel.

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:07.040
This is just a quick mini-video to answer 
a frequently asked question that I get on my channel,

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.340
and that is: "What is a lingua franca?"

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.120
"Lingua franca" is a phrase 
that I use a lot on the channel.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:18.600
A lingua franca is a language that is used
 for communication between people 
who speak different native languages.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:23.280
Here are a couple of example situations 
of when a lingua franca is necessary.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:28.280
For example, there are many languages spoken 
in a particular area or amongst different ethnic groups,

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:30.800
and they need a language 
to communicate with one another.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:35.320
Another example: there is trade between 
two different countries that have no common language.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:42.020
Example three: at a professional conference for experts 
from different countries who have different native languages.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:45.900
On a global scale, the current largest
 lingua franca is English,

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.440
but there are other various lingua francas
on a regional level and on a national level.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:54.800
An example of a regional lingua franca 
is the French language in Africa.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:59.840
There are a lot of different languages spoken in Africa, 
and in a large portion of the continent, they use French

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:02.700
as their language of communication, 
as their lingua franca.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:09.140
An example of a national lingua franca is, in Pakistan, 
where there are various different languages spoken,

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:13.720
but Urdu, the national language, is used 
as the lingua franca between those different goups.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:18.560
Another similar situation is in Philippines, 
where they have two lingua francas, kind of,

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.500
or they mix the two lingua francas sometimes. 
They have Tagalog and English,

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:27.840
and those are used as the lingua francas 
between people from different regions of the country.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:33.740
Sometimes, there are both national lingua francas 
and more local lingua francas within a country.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:39.080
So, if we take the example of Philippines, 
there is a region where Ilocano is the main language.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:44.880
So, if someone from the Ilocano-speaking region meets 
someone from, for example, the Bisaya-speaking region,

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:50.360
they'll probably use the national lingua franca, 
which is Tagalog or English, or use both of them.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:56.000
But if that same person meets someone from the 
Ilocano-speaking region who speaks a different language,

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.620
like a minority language, then they'll probably use 
that local language, Ilocano, as the lingua franca.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:05.260
Because just in that one region, 
there are various languages spoken,

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:10.220
so the lingua franca you use depends on who 
you're speaking to, so you're sort of leveling up.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:15.220
If you speak to someone in your region, you can
 understand them, use that regional lingua franca.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:19.200
If you speak to someone from a different region, 
use the national lingua franca.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:25.380
So it sounds all kind of complicated,
but basically it means : speak the language that helps 
you communicate with that person the best.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:28.000
Why do we say "lingua franca"?

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:35.660
The term "lingua franca" itself comes from the name 
of a language that was spoken in this way 
in the Mediterranean region for hundreds of years.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:42.240
It was kind of a creole Romance language (*Actually, 
PIDGIN not creole*)  that had a lot of vocabulary from
different Mediterranean languages mixed into it.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:48.260
One English source from 1632 referred 
to this language as "Bastard Spanish."

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:58.040
So, to wrap up and clarify, a lingua franca is 
a language that is used for communication 
between the speakers of different native languages.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:02.020
I hope that clarifies things. 
Thank you for watching. Have a nice day.

